# A non-scalar payload is compared STRUCTURALLY: an `[int]` VALUE is not an
# `[u32]`, so `Option([u32])` cannot hold it and nothing widens on the way in.
# Both compilers must reject. (Locks check_sum_leaf's non-literal payload
# comparison.)
#
# HISTORY, and read this before "simplifying" the payload back to a literal: it
# used to be `Some([1])`, under a comment claiming "there is no element-literal
# adaptation inside an array (only a bare `[]` and a top-level scalar literal
# adapt)". That rule was never true of the compiler -- the FIXED and BOUNDED
# destinations have pushed their element type into each element since 1.6
# (src/tychoc.c:6483, src/tychoc.c:6495), so `a: [3]u32 = [1, 2, 3]` has always
# compiled. Only the dynamic `[u32]` was left out; the loops-cleanup plan closed that
# gap and docs/spec/06-conversions.md:27 now states the rule. `Some([1])`
# therefore compiles today, so pinning it here was pinning an accident. What
# this fixture is FOR -- a value that already has a type is not re-typed by an
# annotation -- is untouched by that change, and is what it tests now.
#   error: declared type Option([u32]) but value is Some([int])
fn main():
    xs := [1]
    x : Option([u32]) = Some(xs)
    println("unreachable")
